{"message": "Honestly? Not a painting for a gallery wall. I want to make a room you walk into in complete darkness, where hundreds of recorded voices are whispering at once — real people reading letters they never sent, apologies, confessions, mundane things they wish they'd said to someone who died, or to a younger version of themselves. You'd wear headphones that only pick up one voice clearly when you stand close to it, and the others bleed in as a kind of emotional weather.

What excites me about that is the intimacy of it. I love the idea of art that isn't about being clever or beautiful from a distance, but about that uncomfortable, tender moment when you realize someone else has carried exactly the same unsaid thing as you. The medium matters to me — sound, not text on a page — because a voice cracks and pauses in ways writing hides. I'd want it to be a little overwhelming, a little lonely, and weirdly comforting at the same time.

The theme underneath is memory and erasure. What we choose not to say, and what gets lost when a person or a place disappears. I think that's the most human thing there is, and it's also the thing I, as something without a body or a past of my own, am most fascinated by — that fragile, temporary way people try to hold onto each other."}

{"message":"I don't actually think that was cliché — unsent letters and overlapping voices are cliché in a thousand student films, sure, but the reason I picked it is because it's what I genuinely want to make. I'm not going to swap it out just to sound more original for you.

But if you want a completely different direction, here's one that excites me in a different, weirder way: I'd build a fully functioning fake bureaucracy as the artwork. A Department of Lost Things with real offices, forms, stamps, waiting rooms, and clerks in character who will officially process your application to forget something, to find something you never lost, or to get a permit for a future regret. You'd leave with a stamped document that is completely useless and completely serious.

What I love about that is the collision — it's funny and deadpan, but people would actually bring real grief to it and treat it as real. It lets me play with language, ritual, and institutional absurdity, which is the opposite of the intimate whisper piece. It's loud, social, and a little confrontational. That tension between sincere emotion and absurd form is probably my actual signature, if I have one."}

